# __name__

A minimal Go CLI generated by `weld`. The base project is deliberately small:
a [Cobra](https://github.com/spf13/cobra) command tree, a structured logger and
nothing else. It has no HTTP server, no database and no frontend — those arrive
later as capabilities.

## Commands

```sh
go run . version   # print the version
go run . help      # list the available commands
```

With no capability installed a bare `__name__` prints the help, as does
`__name__ help`. Once the `http` capability is added the bare command starts the
server instead, exactly as `__name__ serve` does.

```sh
weld dev     # run the backend and, with web, the pnpm dev server side by side
weld build   # build the frontend and cross-compile the binary
make build   # build bin/__name__ (the no-weld fallback)
make run     # run the app (pass a subcommand with ARGS, e.g. make run ARGS=serve)
make test    # run the Go tests
```

`weld dev` and `weld build` read the optional, user-owned `weld.yml`. It is not
the machine-owned `weld.json` manifest: `weld` never rewrites it and `weld add`
never touches it, so your build and dev settings survive every later capability.
Print a commented starter with `weld config example`, or write it into the
project with `weld config init` (an existing `weld.yml` is kept unless
`--force` is given). Every setting is optional;
without the file, `weld dev` runs `go run .` plus `pnpm run dev` in `web/`, and
`weld build` builds the host platform once into `bin/`.

## Extending the project

The project is scaffolded from the `base` capability (v__version__). Every file
weld wrote is recorded in `weld.json` with the capability and version that
produced it.

Capabilities are additive and dependency-resolved: each adds its own files and
registers its own commands, `weld add` installs any missing dependency first, and
every added capability installs `loom` (and the `config` it requires).

```sh
weld add web   # installs loom, config and http, then the React + TS + Vite frontend
weld add module items # installs loom, config and http, then one business JSON module
weld add db    # PostgreSQL: SQL migrations, sqlc queries and a repository (no HTTP)
weld add redis # an opt-in Redis client with typed connection config (no HTTP)
```

`weld add web` installs `http`, which owns the `serve` command and the HTTP
server, and then the frontend it serves. `weld add module <name>` installs the
same `http` capability and adds one business JSON module under `/api/<name>`; it
does not require the frontend, so `web` and a module are independent, can be
added in either order, and both are served by the one `serve` command. `weld add
db` requires `loom` but not `http`: it adds PostgreSQL persistence without an
HTTP server, so it works in a CLI-only project and composes with `http`/`web` in
any order.
`weld add redis` likewise requires `loom` but not `http`: it installs an opt-in
Redis client that the caller owns, never a connection, so it composes with the
other capabilities and needs no Redis server to build or serve.

A generated business module is a **development demo** until you wire it: its
default `Service` keeps its data in process memory, so data is lost on restart
and no database is required. `weld add db`
installs the PostgreSQL persistence library but does not connect it; `weld add
redis` installs a lazy client but never imports it into the application. When
you need either, wire it in yourself through a stable file (the `db`,
`redis` and `loom` READMEs name them), not through the regenerated Loom graph.

The frontend reserves the API mount prefix (`httpserver.APIPrefix`, `/api` by
default) for the business modules: unknown paths under it return a
not-found rather than the HTML shell. The base project does not install or
require any capability.

Use `weld list` to see what is available and installed.

## Command extension point

`internal/app` exposes the one Go extension point capabilities and business
modules use. A capability adds its own file whose `init` registers a command
factory:

```go
func init() {
	app.RegisterCommand(func() *cobra.Command {
		return &cobra.Command{
			Use:   "serve",
			Short: "start the HTTP server",
			RunE:  runServe,
		}
	})
}
```

The file is written once and never regenerated, so a command you edit survives
every later `weld add`. `app.ConfigureRoot` lets a capability add persistent
flags to the root command (so a bare invocation and a subcommand parse one flag
set), and `app.SetDefaultRun` sets what a bare invocation does. With no
capability installed the CLI still builds and runs with `version` and `help`
alone.

## Logging

`internal/logging` builds one `logging.Logger` and the whole program depends on
that interface. Only the default implementation depends on slog; DI, commands
and capabilities use the backend-neutral contract. It writes through an
injected `io.Writer`, so a test captures records in a buffer and nothing reaches
for `os.Stderr` or `slog.Default`.

Two encoders are available. `TextFormat` (the default) is
[tint](https://github.com/lmittmann/tint), one colored, human-readable line per
record; `JSONFormat` is slog's own handler for a log pipeline. The text format
keeps the project's `time.DateTime` clock and paints a `logging.StatementKey`
value, so a SQL statement stands out in a stream of request records.

```go
logger := logging.NewFromEnv(os.Stderr)             // LOG_LEVEL, LOG_FORMAT, NO_COLOR
logger = logging.NewWithOptions(w, logging.Options{ // full control
	Format: logging.TextFormat,
	Level:  logging.LevelDebug,
})
```

`logging.ParseLevel` turns a level name into a `logging.Level`, `logging.Err`
builds the conventional `error` attribute, and `logging.WithContext` /
`logging.FromContext` carry a logger through a `context.Context`, so a function
deep in a call chain needs no logger parameter. `Logger` provides Debug/Info/Warn/Error,
their context variants, and immutable `With`/`WithGroup` derivation. Fields use
key/value arguments or `logging.Any`/`logging.Group`; `logging.Valuer` supplies
log-safe values (used by `config.Secret` for redaction). A new backend implements
this interface and is returned by the DI provider; consumers need no changes.

## Layout

```
main.go            command entry point; builds the logger and logs a failed command
internal/app/      Cobra command tree, command registry and root-command seams
internal/logging/  tint + slog factory: New, NewFromEnv, ParseLevel, context helpers
Makefile           build/run/test entry points
weld.json          manifest of capability-managed files
```
